Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026
Notes: Period live-action, 1840s. Candle, gaslight and shadow throughout — one medium, no stylization breaks.

FADE IN:

INT. PRISON CELL - NIGHT

A bare stone room. A single grated window high up. THE NARRATOR — gaunt, thirties, black hair damp against his forehead, collarless white shirt, black waistcoat — sits forward on a low bench, wrists loose between his knees. He looks straight into the lens, calm as a clerk.

(SOUND: slow drip of water on stone, a distant iron door closing)

THE NARRATOR
True! — nervous — very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses — not destroyed — not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

He tilts his head, listening to something above the ceiling. Nothing is there.

(SOUND: faint scratch of a rat behind the wall)

THE NARRATOR
I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! — and observe how healthily, how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

A shuttered upstairs room in an old house. Heavy bedstead, brass-bound chest, a tub in the corner. THE OLD MAN — white-haired, thin as kindling, flannel nightgown and a soft nightcap — sits propped against the bolster. His right eye is a pale, filmed blue, dull as skimmed milk.

THE NARRATOR crosses with a bowl of broth and sets it on the stool beside him.

(SOUND: a mantel clock ticking, spoon knocking the rim of the bowl)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

The old man turns his head. The pale eye settles on THE NARRATOR and stays there. THE NARRATOR's hand stops halfway to the bowl.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture — a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

A shutter slat lets in one thin bar of daylight. It crosses the old man's face and lights the eye alone.

(SOUND: the clock's tick, suddenly louder in the small room)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And so by degrees — very gradually — I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. PRISON CELL - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR is on his feet now, shirt sleeves rolled, hair stuck to his temple. He holds up one finger, patient, instructive.

(SOUND: his bare heel scuffing the stone floor)

THE NARRATOR
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded — with what caution — with what foresight — with what dissimulation I went to work!

He smiles, and the smile is fond.

THE NARRATOR
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

INT. UPSTAIRS HALL - NIGHT

Pitch dark but for the shape of a door. THE NARRATOR stands before it, a dark tin lantern shut tight in his left hand. His thumb and finger take the latch and turn it a hair's breadth at a time.

(SOUND: the latch tongue easing back, one metal click stretched thin)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it — oh, so gently!

The door parts. An opening no wider than a head. He slides the closed lantern through first, then his head, by degrees.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And then, when I had made an opening sufficient for my head, I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out, and then I thrust in my head.

Inside the black room, the sleeping shape of the old man resolves out of the dark. Sweat runs along THE NARRATOR's jaw.

(SOUND: the old man's slow breathing; a deathwatch beetle ticking in the wall)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha! — would a madman have been so wise as this?

His fingers work the lantern's tin shutter open a crack.

(SOUND: a hinge whimpering, one small creak)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I undid it just so much that a single thin ray fell upon the vulture eye.

The thread of light lands on the old man's face. The lid is down. Closed. THE NARRATOR's mouth tightens. The shutter closes again.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights — every night just at midnight — but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Morning. THE NARRATOR throws back the shutter bar with a bang and sets a basin down, brisk and hearty. THE OLD MAN, nightcap askew, blinks up at him from the pillow.

(SOUND: shutter clatter, water sloshing in the basin, a sparrow outside)

THE NARRATOR
Good morning to you! And how have you passed the night?

The old man nods, patting the young man's hand. THE NARRATOR wrings out the cloth and gives it to him with a smile of perfect warmth.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
So you see he would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. PRISON CELL - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR crouches by the wall, tracing something invisible on the stone with his fingertip. The damp black hair, the collarless shirt, the waistcoat hanging open.

(SOUND: fingernail dragging on stone)

THE NARRATOR
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch's minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

He looks up into the lens, triumphant.

THE NARRATOR
Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers — of my sagacity. I could scarcely contain my feelings of triumph.

INT. UPSTAIRS HALL - NIGHT

The door creeps inward, hair by hair. THE NARRATOR's eye glitters in the crack. His lips move without sound — a private laugh.

(SOUND: distant church clock striking twelve)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
To think that there I was, opening the door, little by little, and he not even to dream of my secret deeds or thoughts. I fairly chuckled at the idea; and perhaps he heard me.

Beyond the door, bedclothes shift suddenly. The old man's shape stirs as if startled.

(SOUND: rope bedframe creaking)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
His room was as black as pitch with the thick darkness, for the shutters were close fastened, through fear of robbers; and so I knew that he could not see the opening of the door, and I kept pushing it on steadily, steadily.

His head is inside now. His thumb moves to the lantern's tin fastening — and slips.

(SOUND: a bright metallic snap)

THE OLD MAN
Who's there?

THE NARRATOR does not breathe. He does not move. The lantern hangs dead still in his fist.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

Sweat gathers at the end of his nose and falls. In the dark room the old man sits bolt upright, a grey silhouette, head turned toward the door.

(SOUND: deathwatches ticking in the wall, steady as a pulse)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief — oh, no! — it was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe. I knew the sound well.

The old man's hands knot in the coverlet. He whispers to himself, coaxing.

THE OLD MAN
It is nothing but the wind in the chimney — it is only a mouse crossing the floor.

He listens. He does not lie down.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
All in vain; because Death, in approaching him, had stalked with his black shadow before him, and enveloped the victim.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Blackness. Then a crevice of light opens in the lantern's tin face and one dim ray, fine as spider's thread, crosses the room and stops on the old man's eye.

It is open. Wide, wide open. Dull blue, hideously veiled.

(SOUND: a held breath; then a low, dull, quick beat, like a watch wrapped in cotton)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open — wide, wide open — and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. All a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it that chilled the very marrow in my bones.

Nothing else of the old man is lit. Only the spot.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The ray trembles. THE NARRATOR steadies his wrist with his other hand and holds it.

(SOUND: the beat quickening, thickening, filling the room)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage. Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

His jaw clenches. His eyes flick to the shuttered window, to the wall, to the neighbours beyond it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I thought the heart must burst. And now a new anxiety seized me — the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

He yells and flings the lantern wide. Light floods the room. He leaps in.

(SOUND: a single shriek, cut off)

The old man is dragged down out of the bed. THE NARRATOR heaves the heavy bedstead over onto him and leans his whole weight upon it, grinning.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done. But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

He sits on the overturned bed, hands folded, patient as a man waiting for a kettle. The candle in the lantern gutters.

(SOUND: the muffled beat, slower, slower, under the boards of the bed)

A stray feather drifts down through the light and settles. The beat stops. Silence.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

He tips the bed off, kneels, and lays his palm flat on the old man's chest. He holds it there. And holds it.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. PRISON CELL - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR stands square to the lens, sleeves rolled to the elbow, black hair plastered flat, waistcoat unbuttoned. He is pleased with himself.

(SOUND: water dripping; a guard's boots passing and fading in the corridor)

THE NARRATOR
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The lantern set on the floor, its light low. A tub has been pulled into the middle of the room. THE NARRATOR works with his back to us, silent, methodical, his shadow huge on the wall. A saw is laid down on a folded sheet.

(SOUND: a slow rasp of steel, then nothing but the man's steady breathing)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence. First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

He prises up three floorboards with an iron bar and lowers his burden down between the joists.

(SOUND: nails groaning out of old wood)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then took up three planks from the flooring of the chamber, and deposited all between the scantlings. I then replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye — not even his — could have detected any thing wrong.

He kneels, taps the boards home, and runs his palm across them. The floor is a floor again. He looks at his own dry hands.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out — no stain of any kind — no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that. A tub had caught all — ha! ha!

A bell begins to strike, far off. Four o'clock. Still dark as midnight. Then — three heavy knocks from below.

(SOUND: church bell tolling four; fists hammering a street door)

INT. ENTRANCE HALL - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR comes down the stairs with a candle, quite light of step, and draws the bolt. Three men fill the doorway in dark caps and greatcoats, brass shining at the collar: OFFICER DUNNE, broad and grey-whiskered; OFFICER TALBOT, young, hat in hand; OFFICER MERRICK, lean, with a lantern of his own.

(SOUND: bolt sliding, night wind gusting into the hall)

OFFICER DUNNE
Officers of the police, sir. A shriek was heard by a neighbour in the night. Information has been lodged of foul play, and we are deputed to search the premises.

THE NARRATOR smiles and stands wide of the door to let them by.

THE NARRATOR
Gentlemen, you are welcome. The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Search — search well.

He takes up the candle and leads them through the hall, past the shut parlour door, and up the stairs, holding the light back for them like a host.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR throws the brass-bound chest open and shows them the old man's treasures, secure and undisturbed. OFFICER MERRICK's lantern swings over the walls, the bedstead, the floor.

(SOUND: coins shifting in the chest, boots crossing bare boards)

Bold as brass, THE NARRATOR carries in three chairs and sets them down for the officers to rest. He places his own seat squarely over the three replaced planks and sits upon it.

They sit. They chat of familiar things. He answers cheerily.

(SOUND: chair legs settling, low easy conversation, a match struck)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease. But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

His fingers press his temple. OFFICER DUNNE says something pleasant that we barely hear.

(SOUND: a thin ringing rising under the voices)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct — it continued and gained definiteness — until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

He talks faster, louder, hands moving. His face has gone white as tallow.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound — much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton. I gasped for breath — and yet the officers heard it not.

(SOUND: the muffled beat, unmistakable now, coming up through the floor)

He stands and argues about trifles in a high key, gesticulating. OFFICER TALBOT glances up at him, amused. THE NARRATOR paces, heavy strides, back and forth across the planks.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I foamed — I raved — I swore! I swung the chair upon which I had been sitting, and grated it upon the boards, but the noise arose over all and continually increased.

He drags the chair screeching across the floor. The three officers go on smiling, nodding, at their ease.

(SOUND: chair grating on wood; the beat swelling louder — louder — louder)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
And still the men chatted pleasantly, and smiled. Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God! — no, no! They heard! — they suspected! — they knew! — they were making a mockery of my horror!

He claws at his own collar. The smiles hang in the lamplight.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I could bear those hypocritical smiles no longer! I felt that I must scream or die!

He hurls the chair aside and drops to his knees on the boards, tearing at the seam with his nails.

THE NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed! — tear up the planks! — here, here! — It is the beating of his hideous heart!

The officers are on their feet. OFFICER MERRICK's lantern swings down over the floor.

(SOUND: nails scrabbling on wood; the beat hammering to a stop)

INT. PRISON CELL - NIGHT

THE NARRATOR sits again on the low bench — gaunt, black hair damp, collarless shirt, black waistcoat — hands quiet on his knees. The high window is grey with coming morning.

(SOUND: dripping water; the drag of a chain)

THE NARRATOR
They heard! — they suspected! — they knew! This I thought, and this I think.

He turns his head slowly toward the stone floor and listens. Beneath the flags, faint and patient, the low, dull, quick sound begins again. He does not look up.

(SOUND: a watch wrapped in cotton, beating under the stone)

FADE OUT.

THE END
